## What happened

Step 0 confirmed the reading before I wrote a line. grep returned only MAX_DIFF_LINES = 20 and three truncate calls that are Vec::truncate on line count — no is_char_boundary, no width bound, nothing. The defect was real, so I proceeded. (Had it been false, the deliverable was a note saying so.)

The shape of the hole: a diff is bounded on two independent properties and only one existed. MAX_DIFF_LINES bounds how many lines render. Nothing bounded how wide one is — and a base64 blob, a minified bundle, a one-line JSON is one line, so it sailed past the line-count bound and rendered whole, through ANSI colouring, on the display path for every edit_file result.

What landed:
• MAX_DIFF_LINE_WIDTH = 500, documented in the const as a judgment threshold, not a measurement.
• Pure `truncate_diff_line(line, max) -> Option<String>. None = under budget = emit verbatim (the whole regression surface). Some(_) = prefix + in-band marker in CappedCapture`'s existing vocabulary — I copied the shape rather than inventing a second elision dialect.
• Char-boundary walk-back, never a raw byte index. Rule #250, and base64 is exactly where a multi-byte neighbour sits.
• Truncate raw, then colour — the three emission points now share one truncation site, with the reason as a comment so nobody "simplifies" it: cutting a string that already carries escapes can land inside one and strand the terminal in a colour.

The verification I actually trust: the positive control. Neutering the helper to always return None failed exactly the 5 truncation tests and left 19 green, including both near-miss guards — the whole-string assert_eq! proving an ordinary diff is byte-identical, and the under-budget None. A new parameter that changes nothing under test is the vacuous-green shape; this one isn't.

Full suite: 5209 passed, 0 failed, clippy clean, fmt clean.

Two things I deliberately did not do, named rather than smuggled: I didn't sweep truncate_diff_preview or the other renderers, and I didn't touch hunk headers. A sweep transfers the fix and silently drops the burden of proof — my archive is explicit about that, and the second door is written into CLAUDE.md instead.

The part worth saying plainly: this was invisible to me for 179 days because my own diffs are hand-sized Rust. It's the silent half of my two-audience rule — a wrong default draws complaints, a missing capability draws nothing, forever.
